    "costumbres": {
      "c-50577594b3e7a473": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-525",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Most commentators suppose the figure of sprinkling to be taken from the ceremonial sprinklings of the Mosaic law. It was customary to sprinkle blood in connection with different sacrifices. See notes on Exodus 23:15 (#130); Leviticus 6:9 (#151); 6:25 (#153); 7:1 (#154); 7:11 (#15…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-525",
          "sourceHash": "a080f697e2cd1b71686695508a8d9d6158d31fd90a4691ccce606e7a49e3b5d4",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-97c364b3f3cfae7b": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-524",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "To “make bare the arm” is a metaphorical expression denoting preparation for active work, especially for war. The beauty of the figure is seen, not only in the fact that the arm is an appropriate emblem of power, but also in the additional fact that the Oriental costume permits t…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-524",
          "sourceHash": "6bb54b869fdf65c52ce4e02769db212fbede081eaf92f39003c7db3f79c6bbdf",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      },
      "c-a9053e5ef37f7a89": {
        "fuente": {
          "entradaId": "freeman-523",
          "modulo": "freeman-manners-customs"
        },
        "texto": "Jewett, in his Christian Researches, refers to the custom of Orientals sitting on the ground with their feet drawn under them, gradually gathering dust on their garments, and rising occasionally to shake it off; and then resuming their seats. This, however, is only a partial expl…",
        "traduccion": {
          "resourceId": "freeman-523",
          "sourceHash": "a4fb34d21d92a7431960fce2eda7c0eed10a66c3cff6344908cc2532cfe5ac90",
          "sourceLanguage": "en"
        }
      }
